# FLoRA Extractor — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# Never commit .env to git.
#
# Three files, in precedence order (shared/config.py loads .env then .env.defaults,
# and python-dotenv never overwrites a variable that is already set):
#
#   .env            gitignored — YOUR secrets and per-machine settings.
#   .env.defaults   committed — shared non-secret project identifiers (the Supabase
#                   project, the survivor-pool repo). Do not put secrets there, and
#                   do not restate a shared/config.py default there.
#   shared/config.py  committed — every tunable (rate limits, batch sizes, timeouts,
#                   paths): the default lives there with its rationale, and .env may
#                   override it per machine. This is the whole .env surface in one file.
#
# CONSTANTS that no environment may override — model ids, thresholds, prices, closed
# vocabularies: the things that decide what the pipeline concludes — are not in any of
# these files. Each lives with the code it governs; see "Constants" below for the map.
#
# This file is the catalogue: it names every variable the code reads. Anything the
# code decided is a constant is listed here too, marked as not settable, so you learn
# where it lives instead of setting a line that does nothing.

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your email address — included in API request headers for OpenAlex and Crossref
# (required for polite pool access, higher rate limits)
RESEARCHER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Gemini API key — primary LLM (free tier at aistudio.google.com)
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_key_here

# ── Optional but recommended ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Additional Gemini API keys, tried in order when one returns 429.
# Add as many as you have — keys must be sequential (2, 3, 4, …).
# The pipeline stops loading at the first missing slot, so never leave gaps.
# NOTE: Gemini rate limits are applied PER PROJECT, not per key, so extra keys
# from the same project do NOT raise your quota — they only cover a revoked or
# misconfigured key. Throughput comes from enabling billing (Tier 1), which
# lifts the heavy model from 20 to 10,000 requests/day.
GEMINI_API_KEY_2=
GEMINI_API_KEY_3=
# GEMINI_API_KEY_4=
# GEMINI_API_KEY_5=
# GEMINI_API_KEY_6=  (keep adding as needed — no code changes required)

# OpenAI API key — REQUIRED for Stage 3 with the default SCREEN_VOTER2_MODEL
# (gpt-5.4-mini is called on OpenAI direct), and the middle rung of the linking ladder.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# OpenAlex API key — optional, recommended for Stage 3.
# Without it: polite pool via mailto= parameter — works fine for normal usage.
# With it: higher rate limits + authenticated access to content.openalex.org
# (the GROBID XML endpoint used by Tier 0 PDF acquisition in Stage 3).
# Get one at https://openalex.org or via your institution's OpenAlex access.
OPENALEX_API_KEY=

# Further OpenAlex keys, used in rotation. OpenAlex bills per request against a
# per-key daily budget that resets at midnight UTC, so a long Stage 3 run can drain
# one key mid-run and the client moves to the next on a budget refusal. Give either a
# comma-separated list or numbered slots (2…9); duplicates are dropped.
# OPENALEX_API_KEYS=key_one,key_two
# OPENALEX_API_KEY_2=

# Semantic Scholar API key (Stage 1). Request one at
# https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api#api-key-form — free, but human-reviewed,
# so it can take days. Without it the S2 abstract-backfill tier shares one throttled
# anonymous pool; with it you get 1 req/sec of your own, which is what makes the
# 500-DOI batching worth ~50 DOIs/sec.
S2_API_KEY=
# SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_KEY= — accepted as an alias for the line above.

# SerpAPI keys — Google Scholar tier of the PDF acquisition waterfall. Optional;
# without them that tier is skipped. The second key takes over on 429/quota errors.
SERPAPI_KEY=
SERPAPI_KEY_2=

# Elsevier Scopus API key — optional abstract-backfill tier (Stage 1 fetch_abstracts
# Phase 4). Backfills abstracts by DOI via the Scopus Abstract Retrieval API.
# Weekly quota is ~10,000 requests; fetch_abstracts caps a run with --scopus-limit
# (default 9000) so one run cannot exhaust it. Get a key at https://dev.elsevier.com.
ELSEVIER_API_KEY=
# Elsevier institutional token — needed when Scopus rejects the key from off-campus.
# ELSEVIER_INSTTOKEN=

# OSF personal access token — OPTIONAL. The registrations endpoint the backfill's
# OSF source reads is public; a token only raises the throttle (OSF meters
# anonymous callers per hour and token-bearing ones per day). Lower OSF_RATE_SEC
# from its 1.0s default once one is set. Get one at https://osf.io/settings/tokens.
# OSF_TOKEN=

# OpenRouter API key — the last provider in the linking ladder (Gemini → OpenAI →
# OpenRouter), so it answers when both of the others fail. Get a key at openrouter.ai —
# free credits available. It is also REQUIRED for Stage 3 if you point
# SCREEN_VOTER2_MODEL at an OpenRouter model id (one containing "/").
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=


# Which keys Stage 3 needs depends on SCREEN_VOTER2_MODEL, the screen's second voter
# (a constant in shared/config.py — see "Constants" below). It is gpt-5.4-mini, on
# OpenAI direct, so run_extract refuses to start without GEMINI_API_KEY and
# OPENAI_API_KEY (unless --no-llm). Point that constant at an OpenRouter id — one
# containing "/" — and OPENROUTER_API_KEY becomes the required one instead.

# ── The cheap discard-only tier — nothing to set ─────────────────────────────
# Two very small models that may only DISCARD a row, and only when both agree it is
# clearly out of scope; a single keep, an unreadable reply or an API failure passes the
# row through unchanged. Its providers are reached with OPENROUTER_API_KEY above.
#
# There is no on/off switch, deliberately: which rows get the tier is a Stage 2 ROUTING
# decision (the `screen_cheap` pile, run by filter/engine/tiers.py). A global flag would
# have applied the cheap gate to rows the rule book routed to the expensive tier. The
# voter models and the minimum-abstract threshold are constants at the top of
# shared/prescreen.py. Evidence on its precision: analysis/prescreen_eval/REPORT.md.

# ── Snapshot scan (Stage 1: search/snapshot_scan.py) ─────────────────────────
# The bulk-parquet scan over the whole OpenAlex corpus. These are machine settings —
# a 400 GB scan behaves differently on a laptop and on the AWS runner.
#
# Rows per pyarrow batch. Survivors are merged per batch, never per file, so this is
# also the ceiling on how much of a large partition is ever held in memory. Lower it
# on a small machine; raise it where there is RAM to spare.
# SNAPSHOT_BATCH_ROWS=50000
# Attempts per partition file before it is skipped and reported at the end of the run.
# SNAPSHOT_HTTP_RETRIES=3
# SNAPSHOT_HTTP_TIMEOUT=60
# The public S3 bucket holding the corpus. Point this at a mirror or a local copy to
# scan without egress; the default is OpenAlex's own bucket.
# FLORA_SNAPSHOT_BASE_URL=https://openalex.s3.amazonaws.com/data/parquet
# The pool's on-disk compression is NOT settable (zstd, a constant in
# search/snapshot_scan.py) — it is a shared format, not a preference.

# ── Snapshot survivor pool sharing (search/pool_sync.py) ─────────────────────
# Hugging Face token + private dataset repo the Stage A survivor pool is shared
# through, so collaborators pull the pool instead of re-running a 13-21 hour scan.
# Token: https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens (read to pull, write to push).
HF_TOKEN=
# The repo itself is set for everyone in the committed .env.defaults; set this only
# to point at a different one.
# FLORA_POOL_REPO=my-org/flora-survivor-pool
# Local pool directory (default: cache/snapshot_pool). Point at a bigger disk if needed.
# FLORA_POOL_DIR=
# Text-overlay directory (default: cache/engine/overlay). The engine's route/export/
# screen/handoff read it automatically whenever it holds overlay chunks.
# FLORA_OVERLAY_DIR=
# Files per Hugging Face commit when pushing (default 100 — one commit per file would
# take the repo past the few-thousand-commit mark where HF says repo UX degrades).
# FLORA_HF_COMMIT_BATCH=
# Concurrent file downloads when pulling the pool (default 8). A pull is latency-
# bound — each file waits on an auth + CDN round trip before its first byte — so
# one file at a time leaves most of the link idle. Lower on a metered connection.
# FLORA_HF_PULL_WORKERS=

# ── Filter-engine LLM tiers (filter/engine/tiers.py) ──────────────────────────
# How many works a `screen` run judges at once (default 8). The per-provider rate
# limit above is what bounds the request rate; this only buys overlap.
# ENGINE_TIER_WORKERS=
# Set false to keep a tier run off Hugging Face entirely. The response blobs are
# still written to cache/engine/responses/ and their verdicts read
# response_pending_upload, which a later run reconciles.
# ENGINE_TIER_HF_UPLOAD=

# ── Stage 3 row loop (extract/run_extract.py) ────────────────────────────────
# How many filtered.csv rows Stage 3 has in flight at once (default 4). Rows are
# independent and each is minutes of provider and download latency; the per-service
# rate limits are what bound the request rate, so this only buys overlap. 1 runs the
# loop straight through with no pool at all.
# EXTRACT_WORKERS=

# ── Supabase (validation tables + filter-engine state authority) ─────────────
# SUPABASE_URL is set for everyone in the committed .env.defaults — do not repeat it
# here unless you are pointing at a different project. You need the service-role key
# (Settings → API → service_role, not the anon key) for the dashboard's Validation
# tab, csv_to_db, and any filter-engine command that claims rows.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
# Supabase Management API personal access token. Read by the Supabase CLI, not by any
# code here — needed only by whoever applies db/migrations/*.sql.
# https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens
# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── Optional infrastructure ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# GROBID server URL — used for reference extraction from PDFs
# Run locally: docker run -t --rm -p 8070:8070 lfoppiano/grobid:0.8.0
# Leave empty to skip GROBID (PDF reference extraction will be limited)
GROBID_URL=http://localhost:8070

# ── Constants — NOT set here, in any file ─────────────────────────────────────
# These are not environment variables anywhere. They decide what the pipeline
# concludes, they are folded into cache keys, and the evaluations under analysis/ are
# evidence about these exact values — so changing one is a commit, and the repo
# records who changed the classifier and when. Each lives with the code it governs:
#
#   shared/config.py         GEMINI_MODEL / _LIGHT / _HEAVY, OPENAI_MODEL,
#                            OPENROUTER_HEAVY_MODEL, SCREEN_VOTER2_MODEL,
#                            GEMINI_THINKING_LEVEL, CURATED_SOURCES
#   shared/prescreen.py      PRESCREEN_VOTER1_MODEL / _VOTER2_MODEL,
#                            PRESCREEN_MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS
#   extract/code_outcome.py  OUTCOME_FULLTEXT_ESCALATION
#   filter/engine/tiers.py   the dry run's price list
#   search/snapshot_scan.py  SNAPSHOT_POOL_COMPRESSION
#   shared/grobid.py         GROBID_RATE_SEC
#   shared/pdf_sources.py    UNPAYWALL_RATE_SEC

# ── Gemini flex inference ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Flex costs 50% less than standard — the same discount as Batch, with no batch
# plumbing — but calls may queue for up to 15 minutes. Paid (billing-enabled)
# keys only; it is applied to every Gemini call, including the PDF and image ones.
# GEMINI_USE_FLEX=true
# GEMINI_FLEX_TIMEOUT=900   # seconds; 900 = 15 minutes (Google's stated maximum)
# Which key SLOTS are paid, by 1-based number (GEMINI_API_KEY = 1, GEMINI_API_KEY_2 = 2,
# …) — slot numbers, not key values. Flex is applied only on these; free-tier keys fall
# back to standard inference.
# GEMINI_PAID_KEY_SLOTS=1

# ── OpenAI flex inference ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The same trade on the metered provider: 50% off standard pricing, calls may queue.
# A model or queue that will not serve flex is answered at standard tier instead.
# OPENAI_USE_FLEX=true
# OPENAI_FLEX_TIMEOUT=900   # seconds; must cover the queueing worst case

# ── Gemini thinking level (heavy model only) ──────────────────────────────────
# "minimal" or "high"; unset keeps the model's own default, which is what the
# answers already in cache/ were produced under. Thinking tokens are billed as
# output, so "minimal" is a large saving on the heavy model — but it changes the
# answers, so set it only after a quality spot-check of linking and outcome coding.
# The value is folded into every cache key naming the heavy model, so switching it
# re-asks rather than replaying answers from the other setting.
# GEMINI_THINKING_LEVEL=minimal

# ── Rate limits (seconds between calls) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Each LLM interval is charged per provider, against that provider's own last-call
# timestamp: the screen's two votes go to different providers and neither waits on
# the other. Raise a value if a provider starts returning 429s.
# GEMINI_RATE_SEC=1.0
# OPENAI_RATE_SEC=0.5
# OPENROUTER_RATE_SEC=0.5
# OPENALEX_RATE_SEC=0.3
# Europe PMC (search/fetch_abstracts.py) is keyless and public — keep this polite.
# EPMC_RATE_SEC=0.4
# CROSSREF_RATE_SEC=0.1
# S2_RATE_SEC=0.5
# SCOPUS_RATE_SEC=1.0

# ── Abstract backfill: batch sizes and quota caps ──────────────────────────────
# Per-call id limits are set by each API; lower them only to be gentler.
# OA_BATCH_SIZE=50
# EPMC_BATCH_SIZE=25
# S2_BATCH_SIZE=500
# Seconds between S2 batches. 5.0 was measured against the live API — see the note
# in shared/config.py before changing it.
# S2_BATCH_RATE_SEC=5.0
# Max Scopus calls per run; Elsevier's quota is ~10k/week.
# SCOPUS_DEFAULT_LIMIT=9000

# ── Daily OpenAI token budget ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hard ceiling on OpenAI tokens (prompt + completion) bought per calendar day — the
# metered spend here. The running total is kept in cache/token_usage.json, so it
# survives a restart and is shared by concurrent runs; once it is reached the
# pipeline stops cleanly instead of calling again. Set to 0 to lift the cap — that
# is the only way to disable it.
# Gemini and OpenRouter usage is recorded in the same file but is never capped.
# OPENAI_DAILY_TOKEN_BUDGET=8000000

# ── Outcome extraction — NOT set here ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The outcome step classifies from the ABSTRACT; when that returns
# cannot_be_determined (or there is no abstract) and parsed fulltext is available, it
# escalates to a second, fulltext-based call. OUTCOME_FULLTEXT_ESCALATION is a
# constant in extract/code_outcome.py: turning it off changes what a row is coded as,
# so it must not vary by machine.

# ── Cache location (fix for slow disks) ────────────────────────────────────────
# cache/ holds hundreds of thousands of small per-identifier files. Random reads of
# pre-existing files scattered across one huge flat directory are a classic bad case
# for a spinning hard disk (each read costs a real seek) — measured ~80x slower than
# an SSD on a 798k-file cache directory, enough to stall a large abstract/PDF backfill
# for hours before it shows any progress. If your repo checkout sits on an HDD, point
# this at a directory on an SSD instead; leave unset for the default (cache/ inside
# the repo).
# FLORA_CACHE_DIR=C:/flora-cache

# ── Monitoring app ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Flask session key. The default is a fixed development string — set a real value
# for any deployment reachable by anyone else.
# FLASK_SECRET_KEY=

# FLORA_READONLY=1 registers only the dashboard and check pages, skipping the
# blueprints that import the heavy PDF dependencies (pdfminer, pymupdf, playwright).
# Use it for read-only hosting where those packages are not installed.
# FLORA_READONLY=1
